"{\n  \"critic_suggestions\": \"The three points and their annotations exactly match the raw data: (0, 9), (1.5, 2.25), and (3, 0). The labels are correct and readable, with no visible overlap, clipping, redundant legend, embedded caption, or unsupported interpolation. The export needs correction: the supplied JPEG measures 1917 \u00d7 1438 pixels, approximately 4:3, rather than the specified 2700 \u00d7 1800 pixels, 3:2. Render directly at the required dimensions without stretching the existing image; use lossless PNG for crisp text and markers.\",\n  \"revised_description\": \"Create fig_intercept_error in R using ggplot2 as a publication-ready scatter plot containing exactly three recorded results. Initialize the complete dataset explicitly as data.frame(b = c(0, 1.5, 3), mse = c(9, 2.25, 0)). Map numeric b to horizontal position and numeric mse to vertical position. Plot exactly (0, 9), (1.5, 2.25), and (3, 0). Do not include connecting lines, fitted curves, interpolation, uncertainty intervals, additional observations, observation-level data, or MAE values.\\n\\nRender directly on a 9 \u00d7 6 inch landscape canvas. Export as lossless PNG using ggsave(filename = 'fig_intercept_error.png', plot = fig_intercept_error, width = 9, height = 6, units = 'in', dpi = 300, bg = 'white'). Verify that the saved image measures exactly 2700 \u00d7 1800 pixels with a 3:2 aspect ratio. Do not stretch, crop, or resample the supplied JPEG to obtain these dimensions. Inspect the final export for clipping and overlap.\\n\\nUse white plot and panel backgrounds and DejaVu Sans throughout. Set outer margins to 18 pt top, 24 pt right, 16 pt bottom, and 18 pt left.\\n\\nUse geom_point() with shape 21, size 4.2, stroke 0.6, fill #0072B2, outline #004A73, and full opacity. Draw markers above the grid. Marker appearance must not encode another variable.\\n\\nLabel the horizontal axis 'Added intercept correction b' and the vertical axis 'Mean squared error'. Keep both scales linear and continuous. Set horizontal breaks exactly at 0, 1.5, and 3, displayed as those strings. Set vertical breaks at 0, 2.25, 4.5, 6.75, and 9, without unnecessary trailing zeros. Use horizontal viewing limits of \u22120.35 to 3.35 and vertical viewing limits of \u22120.6 to 10.2, with no additional expansion. The space below zero provides marker clearance and does not represent negative MSE measurements.\\n\\nRender axis titles at 13 pt, regular weight, in #222222, with 10 pt separation from tick labels. Render tick labels horizontally at 11 pt in #222222. Draw bottom and left axis lines in #6B7280 with 0.4 mm linewidth and outward ticks of 3 pt with 0.35 mm linewidth. Omit top and right borders. Show only horizontal major gridlines at the specified vertical ticks, using #E5E7EB and 0.3 mm linewidth. Omit vertical and minor gridlines.\\n\\nAnnotate the points with the exact strings '(0, 9)', '(1.5, 2.25)', and '(3, 0)'. Place their anchors at (0, 9.45), (1.5, 2.70), and (3, 0.45), respectively. Use horizontal centering and bottom alignment, equivalent to hjust = 0.5 and vjust = 0 in geom_text(). Use 12 pt regular text in #1F2937, converting point sizes to the geometry's native units as needed. Keep labels clearly separated from markers.\\n\\nRender the title as 'Recorded Mean Squared Error\\\\nat Three Intercept Corrections', with the indicated deliberate line break. Use 16 pt bold DejaVu Sans in #111827, align the title with the left edge of the plotting panel, and leave 12 pt below it. Do not display a legend.\\n\\nDo not render a caption, footnote, or explanatory paragraph within the image. Keep this manuscript caption separate: 'Three recorded corrections evaluated on the same five synthetic observations used to determine the correction. No stochastic repetitions or held-out evaluation.'\\n\\nCommunicate only the three recorded arithmetic results, without claims about unmeasured corrections or predictive generalization.\"\n}"